day:2 Next.js — Complete Notes: 1. Pages and Routing

1. Introduction to Pages and Routing

In Next.js, routing is the process of navigating between different pages of a web application.

Next.js provides a file-system-based routing system. This means that the folders and files inside the application determine the URL structure.

For example:

app/
├── page.tsx
├── about/
│   └── page.tsx
├── contact/
│   └── page.tsx
└── products/
    └── page.tsx

This creates the following routes:

File	URL
app/page.tsx	/
app/about/page.tsx	/about
app/contact/page.tsx	/contact
app/products/page.tsx	/products
2. App Router

Modern Next.js applications generally use the App Router.

The App Router is based on the app directory.

Basic structure:

my-next-app/
├── app/
│   ├── page.tsx
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   ├── about/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   └── contact/
│       └── page.tsx
├── public/
├── package.json
└── next.config.ts
Important files
page.tsx

Defines a page that can be accessed through a URL.

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    </main>
  );
}
layout.tsx

Defines a shared layout for pages.

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
3. Creating a Basic Page

Suppose we want to create an About page.

Create:

app/about/page.tsx

Code:

export default function AboutPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>About Us</h1>
      <p>Welcome to our About page.</p>
    </main>
  );
}

Now open:

http://localhost:3000/about

The page will be displayed.

4. Creating Multiple Pages

A website can contain multiple pages.

Example:

app/
├── page.tsx
├── about/
│   └── page.tsx
├── services/
│   └── page.tsx
├── contact/
│   └── page.tsx
└── blog/
    └── page.tsx

Routes:

/
 /about
