5. Nested Routing

Next.js supports nested routes using folders.

Example:

app/
└── products/
    ├── page.tsx
    └── mobile/
        └── page.tsx

Routes:

/products
/products/mobile

The mobile/page.tsx file represents the /products/mobile route.

Example:

export default function MobileProducts() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Mobile Products</h1>
      <p>Explore our mobile products.</p>
    </main>
  );
}
6. Navigation Between Pages

Next.js provides the Link component for navigation.

Import it from:

import Link from "next/link";

Example:

import Link from "next/link";


export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Home Page</h1>


      <Link href="/about">
        Go to About
      </Link>
    </main>
  );
}

Clicking the link navigates to:

/about
7. Navigation Menu

A simple navigation menu:

import Link from "next/link";


export default function Navbar() {
  return (
    <nav>
      <Link href="/">Home</Link>
      <Link href="/about">About</Link>
      <Link href="/services">Services</Link>
      <Link href="/contact">Contact</Link>
    </nav>
  );
}

This component can be included in a layout.

8. Dynamic Routes

Dynamic routes are useful when the URL contains a variable value.

For example:

/products/101
/products/102
/products/103

Instead of creating separate files for every product, use:

app/
└── products/
    └── [id]/
        └── page.tsx

The [id] folder represents a dynamic route.

Example:

export default async function ProductPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>;
}) {
  const { id } = await params;


  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Product ID: {id}</h1>
    </main>
  );
}

Now:

/products/101

produces:

Product ID: 101

And:

/products/500

produces:

Product ID: 500
9. Dynamic Route Structure

Example:

app/
└── blog/
    └── [slug]/
        └── page.tsx

URLs can be:

/blog/nextjs-introduction
/blog/react-tutorial
/blog/javascript-basics

Example:

export default async function BlogPost({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>;
}) {
  const { slug } = await params;


  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Blog Post</h1>
      <p>Slug: {slug}</p>
    </main>
  );
}
10. Catch-All Routes

A catch-all route uses:

[...slug]

Example:

app/docs/[...slug]/page.tsx

It can handle:

/docs/nextjs
/docs/nextjs/routing
/docs/nextjs/routing/dynamic-routes

The route parameters contain multiple path segments.

11. Optional Catch-All Routes

Optional catch-all routes use:

[[...slug]]

Example:

app/docs/[[...slug]]/page.tsx

This can match:

/docs
/docs/nextjs
/docs/nextjs/routing

The difference is that the route can also match the parent path without any additional segments.

12. Route Groups

Route groups allow developers to organize application routes without changing the URL.

They use parentheses:

app/
├── (marketing)/
│   ├── about/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   └── contact/
│       └── page.tsx

The routes are:

/about
/contact

The (marketing) folder does not appear in the URL.

This is useful for organizing large applications.

13. Layouts and Routing

A layout is shared between multiple pages.

Example:

app/
├── layout.tsx
├── page.tsx
├── about/
│   └── page.tsx
└── contact/
    └── page.tsx

app/layout.tsx:

export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <header>
          <h2>My Website</h2>
        </header>


        {children}


        <footer>
          <p>Copyright 2026</p>
        </footer>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}

The header and footer can appear across the application's pages.

14. Nested Layouts

Layouts can also be nested.

Example:

app/
├── layout.tsx
└── dashboard/
    ├── layout.tsx
    ├── page.tsx
    └── settings/
        └── page.tsx

The root layout applies to the entire application.

The dashboard layout applies to:

/dashboard
/dashboard/settings

This is useful for dashboards and admin applications.

15. Programmatic Navigation

Sometimes navigation needs to happen after an action such as submitting a form.

Next.js provides useRouter().

Example:

"use client";


import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";


export default function Login() {
  const router = useRouter();


  function handleLogin() {
    router.push("/dashboard");
  }


  return (
    <button onClick={handleLogin}>
      Login
    </button>
  );
}

After clicking the button, the user is navigated to:

/dashboard

Common methods include:

router.push("/dashboard");
router.replace("/dashboard");
router.back();
router.refresh();
16. push() vs replace()
router.push()

Adds a new entry to the browser history.

router.push("/dashboard");

The user can normally press the browser Back button to return.

router.replace()

Replaces the current history entry.

router.replace("/dashboard");

This can be useful after authentication when you don't want the user to return to the login page using Back.

17. Route Parameters

Dynamic route parameters can be accessed through params.

Example:

app/users/[id]/page.tsx

Code:

export default async function UserPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>;
}) {
  const { id } = await params;


  return (
    <main>
      <h1>User: {id}</h1>
    </main>
  );
}

For:

/users/25

the value of id is:

25
18. Search Parameters

URL query parameters can be accessed using searchParams.

Example URL:

/products?category=mobile

A page can read the query value:

export default async function ProductsPage({
  searchParams,
}: {
  searchParams: Promise<{ category?: string }>;
}) {
  const { category } = await searchParams;


  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Products</h1>
      <p>Category: {category}</p>
    </main>
  );
}

Here:

category = mobile
