20. Loading UI

Next.js supports a special loading.tsx file.

Example:

app/
├── dashboard/
│   ├── page.tsx
│   └── loading.tsx

loading.tsx:

export default function Loading() {
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Loading dashboard...</p>
    </div>
  );
}

This provides a loading interface while the route's content is being loaded.

21. Error Handling

Next.js supports an error.tsx file for route-level errors.

Example:

app/
└── dashboard/
    ├── page.tsx
    └── error.tsx

Example:

"use client";


export default function ErrorPage({
  reset,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string };
  reset: () => void;
}) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>


      <button onClick={() => reset()}>
        Try again
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
22. Not Found Page

A route can have a not-found.tsx file.

Example:

app/
├── not-found.tsx
└── products/
    └── [id]/
        └── page.tsx

Example:

export default function NotFound() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>404 - Page Not Found</h1>
      <p>The requested page could not be found.</p>
    </main>
  );
}
23. Redirects

Next.js provides redirect() for server-side navigation.

Example:

import { redirect } from "next/navigation";


export default function Profile() {
  const isLoggedIn = false;


  if (!isLoggedIn) {
    redirect("/login");
  }


  return <h1>Profile</h1>;
}

If the user is not logged in, they are redirected to:

/login
