Wednesday – Static Site Generation (SSG)


Step-by-step Notes

1. What is Static Site Generation (SSG)?
Static Site Generation means the HTML pages are generated at build time.
When you run npm run build, Next.js creates ready HTML files for your pages.
These static files are then served very fast to users.

2. Why use SSG?



AdvantageExplanationVery FastPages are pre-builtBetter SEOFull HTML is available for search enginesLow Server CostNo need to render on every requestHighly ScalableCan be served from CDNGreat for content sitesBlogs, documentation, portfolios

3. When to use SSG?
Use Static Site Generation when:

Content does not change very frequently
You are building a Blog
Documentation website
Portfolio website
Marketing / Landing pages
Product listing pages (when data is not changing every second)


4. How SSG works in Next.js (App Router)
In the App Router, pages are static by default.
If a page does not use dynamic functions (like cookies, headers, or uncached data), Next.js automatically generates it as a static page.

5. Basic Static Page Example
jsx// app/page.js
export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Static Home Page</h1>
      <p>This page is generated at build time.</p>
    </main>
  );
}

6. Fetching Data for Static Pages
jsx// app/posts/page.js

async function getPosts() {
  const res = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts");
  return res.json();
}

export default async function PostsPage() {
  const posts = await getPosts();

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Blog Posts (SSG)</h1>
      <ul>
        {posts.slice(0, 5).map((post) => (
          <li key={post.id}>
            <h3>{post.title}</h3>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}

7. Generating Static Paths (Dynamic Routes)
jsx// app/blog/[id]/page.js

export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const res = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts");
  const posts = await res.json();

  return posts.slice(0, 10).map((post) => ({
    id: post.id.toString(),
  }));
}

async function getPost(id) {
  const res = await fetch(
    `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/${id}`
  );
  return res.json();
}

export default async function BlogPost({ params }) {
  const post = await getPost(params.id);

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.body}</p>
    </main>
  );
}

