Thursday – Server-Side Rendering (SSR)
Step-by-step Notes

1. What is Server-Side Rendering (SSR)?
Server-Side Rendering means the HTML is generated on every request on the server.
When a user visits a page, the server creates the HTML at that moment and sends it to the browser.

2. SSG vs SSR


FeatureSSG (Static)SSR (Server-Side)When HTML is generatedAt build timeOn every requestSpeedVery FastSlightly slowerData FreshnessCan be oldAlways freshServer LoadLowHigherBest forBlogs, PortfoliosDashboards, Real-time data

3. When to use SSR?
Use Server-Side Rendering when:

Data changes very frequently
You need personalized content (user dashboard)
You need real-time data
SEO is important + data must be fresh
Pages depend on cookies, headers, or authentication


4. How to make a page SSR in App Router
In the App Router, a page becomes dynamic (SSR) when you use:

cookies()
headers()
searchParams (in some cases)
fetch with cache: "no-store"
fetch with next: { revalidate: 0 }


5. Basic SSR Example
jsx// app/dashboard/page.js

async function getData() {
  const res = await fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts", {
    cache: "no-store", // This makes it SSR
  });
  return res.json();
}

export default async function Dashboard() {
  const posts = await getData();

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Dashboard (SSR)</h1>
      <p>Data is fetched on every request.</p>
      <ul>
        {posts.slice(0, 5).map((post) => (
          <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  );
}

6. Using searchParams (SSR)
jsx// app/search/page.js

export default async function SearchPage({ searchParams }) {
  const query = searchParams.q || "";

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Search Page (SSR)</h1>
      <p>You searched for: {query}</p>
    </main>
  );
}
URL Example: /search?q=nextjs

7. Using headers and cookies
jsximport { cookies, headers } from "next/headers";

export default async function Page() {
  const cookieStore = cookies();
  const headersList = headers();

  const theme = cookieStore.get("theme")?.value || "light";
  const userAgent = headersList.get("user-agent");

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>SSR with Cookies & Headers</h1>
      <p>Theme: {theme}</p>
      <p>User Agent: {userAgent}</p>
    </main>
  );
}

